"Hello there.  Mr. Frumble's the name, traveling is the game.  My
friend the wizard Thobalt kept talking about the Dungeons of Doom and
all of the mysterious, dangerous, and somewhat unsanitary things that
happened to him whilst retrieving some bauble on a church project.

"Well, I'd never really imagined him as a missionary, but the travel
abroad seemed to have done him a world of good. He seems healthier and
his skin rather glows now, although he gets very touchy when I ask him
about his diet.

"I'd been selling vacuum cleaners around Busytown for a while, and was
getting a bit run down. A spot of vacation in an interesting place
sounded just right, so I hopped in my pickle car and zoomed off to see
Thobalt ("Thobalt the Demigod" he keeps insisting, and after a drink
or two starts waving that glowing dagger, but he's really alright).

"He listened to my vacation idea very attentively, but he wasn't as
helpful as I'd hoped.  Can you believe it, he hadn't any brochures or
maps - not even the simple ones the local tourist bureau hands out.
Even worse, his address book of the people he'd visited was almost
useless - virtually every name was scratched out! He did mention to
say "Hi!" if I ran into some Demogorgon fellow, as they'd apparently
missed each other. Most unbelievably, he didn't have a single picture
from his entire trip.  A photo album really does make the trip, I had
to insist.

"Thobalt did TRY to be helpful, however. He offered me this huge black
sword, a set of books (I suspect they were travelogues), some fancy
jewelry, and this strange silvery suit of armor. When I asked how he
carried all of this stuff, he proudly whipped out this dingy little
sack.

"I smiled and thanked him for the offer, but told him I was sure I'd
pick up lots of local trinkets on my own. Since I'd stopped at the
store and bought "Let's Go: Doom" already, I declined the books as
well.  They wouldn't fit in my luggage, anyway. He seemed rather
astonished, and not too coherent when I later asked about his
recommendations on local cuisine.  Something about "making sure the
bodies were fresh."  I assume that he meant carcasses, like steak
tartare. Well, when in Rome...

"As to other critical issues, like how much money to carry and where
the good shopping spots were, he seemed remarkably uninformed.
"Whatever you can carry" and "Wherever" were his responses.

"Obviously, he simply wasn't an experienced traveler. Looked like I'd
need to do on my own..."


JOURNAL OF MR. FRUMBLE

1)  Oh, this will be exciting. I've packed my best camera, got a print
shirt so I'll blend with the natives (ho! ho!) and be able to avoid
being clipped in the shops.  Busytown Bank came through with the
vacation loan and I've booked a seat on Rodney's Dungeons of Doom
Scenic Tour, so I'm all ready to go.

2)  Well, off to a rocky start.  I was standing at the mouth of this
huge cavern waiting for the tour guide to arrive, when a gust of wind
blew my hat right into the cave!  Now, a hat like that can get into
serious trouble if unattended, so I decided to make a quick dash after
it.  Some silly kitty cat that I'd shared my hamburger with decided to
follow me in.

7)  Whew!  All of this climbing up and down is hard work.  The natives
are very aggressive, and keep trying to sell me daggers and darts and
the like.  It's all I can do to keep them off of me sometimes.
However, Mr. Frumble can be firm when firmness is called for, and
since I brought my own competition dart set, I really have been
bothered less since adopting their quaint custom of trading darts.

15)  I feel so silly.  I've been noodling around for days, and haven't
taken many pictures - always in a rush to get somewhere.  Well, this
huge ferocious THING was coming at me and actually had me rather
alarmed, when I realized that the folks back in Busytown would never
believe me if I told them about the strange creatures I'd seen.
Taking a deep breath, I fished out my camera and got a couple of
excellent shots before the beastie tottered off.  I shall remember to
get more film when I get near a mall.

28)  In this cute little village there was a lovely little temple and
the best souvenir shops!  I bought up all of the handmade candles and
an amazing set of luggage (mine was getting a bit ratty).  It really
saved the old back, if you know what I mean, especially after I wiped
it off with some of the local "holy" polish.

42)  Well, the state of the ruins around here is a bit deplorable. I
must have spent HOURS pushing rocks around to get to a silly little
pendant; and when I return, my cat's run off.  Ungrateful little
bugger - I'd collected some "trippy treats" for him.

73)  Gosh, Thobalt mentioned something about putting wands in bags,
but I didn't know that it would singe my eyebrows like that!  I'm now
missing all of the little maps and amulets, and that nifty wand of
wishing that I only tried two times.  I'll really miss my Mai Tai mix,
however.

90)  It's getting warm down here.

93)  Silly me!  It's Hell.  Quite all right, then.  Perfectly normal.
Great shot of the big red fellow with the whip - can't wait to get
these developed!

100)  Oh, castles!  I love old castles!  Hope nobody minds if I poke
around a bit, maybe take a few pictures...

127)  Found the tour guide.  He started in immediately with me,
criticizing me for missing the tour and messing up his head count.  I
apologized and tried to explain about my hat, but he wouldn't listen.
He just got more agitated, and I got more agitated, and I accidentally
hit him with this bird I'd found on the doorstep and was bringing
inside to discard.  At that point he must have gotten very offended,
because he stopped speaking to me altogether. He did give me this
book, however, so his feelings couldn't have been TOO hurt: he was
probably just putting on an act.  I thanked him anyway, and took the
elevator to the next level.

135)  I KNEW he wasn't that mad!  He came back to talk with me again,
and brought his brother.  I enjoyed the company, but the natives
really have no concept of personal space, if you know what I mean.
Anyway, I needed to get back to Busytown and work, so I left.

136)  Why don't the elevators work?

138)  Wait, I've been here before.

161)  Finally, at the cave entrance.  The travel was fun, but the
natives were a little pushy sometimes in the beginning.  However, now
they seem to run away when I saunter along, so I've finally gotten a
bit of peace and quiet.  All in all, a nice vacation.

162)  This isn't Busytown!

163)  Well, the little local map showed me where the public
transportation is, and it's not far at all!  Rodney showed up again
asking about a damage deposit, and I'm afraid I was a bit short with
him.

165)  Good thing I brought that ring!  I don't know how everyone would
get around without one.  The freak winds here are pretty bad, though.
Guess I'll press on.

171)  Can you believe it!  I just start shucking down to take a swim,
and BANG! I'm in some temple.  Well, this silly pendant that local
shaman gave me has been glowing again, so I looked at it more closely.
On the back it says, "If found, return to the Altar of the Lady.
Reward available."  Heck, there's a sign for an altar right here near
where I came in.  I'll return the trinket.

173)  Like Thobalt said, personal hygiene and customs can vary.  The
fellow accosting me must not have eaten in a week.  However, I wasn't
in the mood to deal with panhandlers right then, so I tossed him a
coin and tried to proceed.  He got sniffy, and I had to use the Wand
of Mace (I guess that what it is - he stopped bothering me right
away!) on him - twice!  Some folks just won't take a hint.

174)  Well, here's an altar, and right by the entrance.  Good sign,
what?  Guess I'll drop off the trinket - but first, could really use
something to drink... Aaah.  Now, where was that thing?  Here it is...

